(Every read of a list a callee widened through a parameter: `'s first`,
 `'s last`, `element N of`, iteration, and a `{...}` hole. LANGUAGE.md states
 that appending, `set element`, `element N of`, `first`/`last`, iteration and
 format interpolation all respect each element's actual type, so one widened
 list has to answer all five the same way. docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #97.)

To 'note whatever' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  append label to noted.

To 'relabel the first' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  set element 1 of noted to label.

a list called noted is [].
'note whatever' of noted and "first entry".
'note whatever' of noted and "second entry".

Print "first: {noted's first}".
Print "last: {noted's last}".
Print "element 2: {element 2 of noted}".
For each entry in noted,
  Print "each: {entry}".
Print noted.

(`set element` through the parameter widens a list the caller had proven
 holds numbers, so the replaced slot reads back as the text it now holds and
 the untouched slots still read back as numbers.)
a list called tally is [1, 2, 3].
'relabel the first' of tally and "one".
Print "tally first: {tally's first}".
Print "tally second: {element 2 of tally}".
